# Project scope

## Client request

Deploy our application (VulnLab) to AWS with restricted access, repeatable updates, monitoring, and recovery.

## Purpose

Extend VulnLab into a reproducible cloud security and DevSecOps
case study, demonstrating deployment, access control, detection,
and recovery.

Vulnlab Source: https://github.com/setzexe/VulnLab

## v1.0 scope

- One AWS region (us-east-1)
- Linux EC2 instance running the remediated VulnLab app.
- Terraform managed infrastructure, networking, and permissions.
- Restricted admin and application access.
- SQLite storage containing (fictional) demo data.
- Runtime secrets supplied separately from source code and images.
- Automated infrastructure and application security checks.
- GitHub Actions deployment with temporary AWS credentials.
- Deployment of an identifiable, approved application image.
- Application security logs, AWS activity review, and one tested alert.
- IAM and network boundary verification.
- Tested rollback and database backup/restore.
- Rebuild instructions, sanitized evidence, and verified teardown.

## Out of scope

- Access to the intentionally vulnerable application.
- Real users, data, or production availability.
- Multiple clouds and high availability.
- A permanent public demonstration environment.

## Security + cost boundaries

- Test only resources I own / am authorized to test.
- Vulnerable version stays local only.
- Review the remediated version before deployment.
- Use separate identities with minimal permissions.
- Keep Terraform state and sensitive information out of Git.
- Use AWS Free plan.
- Create billing notifications.
- Document cleanup before creating resources.

## What makes this project done?

- Recreate the environment from documents and code.
- Deploy the identified app version with automated checks.
- Showcase that a failing security or regression check blocks deployment.
- Show that an expected IAM action succeeds and an unrelated action is denied.
- Trigger an application security alert and explain its cooresponding logs.
- Recover from an unhealthy release and record recovery time.
- Restore a database backup and verify the demo data.
- Publish sanitized evidence and reproducible instructions.
- Remove cloud resources and verify cleanup.
